{"target": "minidb/tokens.py, minidb/nodes.py", "findings": ["minidb/tokens.py: the token `kind` vocabulary actually emitted (\"KEYWORD\", \"IDENT\", \"NUMBER\", \"STRING\", \"OP\", \"PUNCT\") is nowhere in the published Token/tokenize interface, so the parser switches on undocumented strings -> publish the exact kind set, plus the rule that KEYWORD values are upper-cased while IDENT values keep source case, as part of the tokens contract.", "minidb/tokens.py: tokenize emits no end-of-input sentinel, so any parser lookahead past the last token raises IndexError, which the spec forbids from leaving the public API -> append a final Token(\"EOF\", \"\", len(sql)) or state in the interface that the parser must bounds-check every peek and api.py must wrap IndexError as QueryError.", "minidb/tokens.py: NULL arrives as Token(\"KEYWORD\", \"NULL\", pos), not as a literal token, while nodes.Literal documents SQL NULL as Python None -> document that the parser must translate KEYWORD \"NULL\" into nodes.Literal(None); otherwise NULL never reaches the engine as None and every IS NULL / NULL comparison silently changes meaning.", "minidb/tokens.py: `-` is only ever emitted as OP and nodes.Select.limit/offset are typed `int | None`, so `LIMIT -1` cannot be represented as parsed and a UnaryOp there would be a type mismatch -> have the parser fold a leading minus into the int it stores in Select.limit/offset so the planner sees -1 and raises QueryError for a negative LIMIT/OFFSET.", "minidb/tokens.py: any word matching a keyword in any case becomes a KEYWORD token, so a case-sensitive identifier such as a column named `order`, `In` or `Is` can never be referenced -> either treat words as keywords only where the grammar expects them, or publish the reserved-word list so the parser reports it as a clear QueryError.", "minidb/nodes.py: SelectItem.source_text defaults to \"\", so an unaliased non-column expression built without it silently gets output name \"\" instead of the whitespace-stripped source text the naming rule requires -> drop the default so the parser must supply source_text, or have the planner raise QueryError when output_name() is empty for a non-Star item.", "minidb/nodes.py: an ORDER BY reference to an output alias is indistinguishable from a column reference, since both arrive as Column(name=..., table=None) -> the planner must resolve an unqualified OrderKey column against select-list output names first and only then against table columns, otherwise a legal `ORDER BY n` becomes unknown-column, or ambiguous-column under a join.", "minidb/nodes.py: BinOp.right overloads a single Expr with a tuple for IN only, and Func('COUNT', (Star(),)) puts a Star in an argument position -> the engine must branch on `isinstance(right, tuple)` for IN and special-case COUNT(*) before evaluating arguments; state both invariants in the nodes contract so the engine does not hit TypeError iterating a non-tuple right or try to evaluate Star as a value.", "minidb/nodes.py: Func.name is documented as upper-cased but nothing enforces it, and functions.py keys SCALARS/AGGREGATES strictly on upper-case names with membership tested by the caller -> the parser must upper-case the IDENT before constructing Func, and the planner must reject an unknown name with QueryError rather than letting the engine do SCALARS[name] and raise KeyError.", "minidb/nodes.py: UnaryOp.op admits \"+\" and \"-\" as well as NOT/IS NULL/IS NOT NULL, but no operator table is published anywhere -> fix the exact op spellings (including whether it is \"IS NULL\" with one space) in the nodes contract, since the engine must dispatch on those strings and an unexpected spelling would fall through to a KeyError or a silent wrong answer."]}
